      "number": 30,
      "title": "Four Names",
      "content": "📖 Opening Quote\n\"A list of names is not the same as a list of suspects.\"\n\nDaniel returned to his office with the report in his briefcase.\n\nHe pinned a fresh sheet of paper to the investigation board.\n\nAt the top, he wrote:\n\nPeople with Administrative Access\n\nBelow it were four names.\n\nProfessor Helen Morris.\n\nMichael Reed, IT Administrator.\n\nSamuel Briggs, Security Supervisor.\n\nAnd...\n\nNathan Ross, Student Assistant.\n\nDaniel looked at the board for a long moment.\n\nHaving access didn't mean having guilt.\n\nIt simply meant they needed to ask more questions.\n\nThe next morning...\n\nExams continued across Westbridge University.\n\nThe campus was quieter than usual.\n\nStudents hurried into lecture halls with anxious expressions.\n\nMarcus looked at Ariana.\n\n\"If I don't come out after this exam...\"\n\n\"Tell my mother I loved her.\"\n\nLena rolled her eyes.\n\n\"It's a two-hour economics paper.\"\n\n\"Not a battlefield.\"\n\nEveryone laughed.\n\nEven Ethan couldn't hide his smile.\n\nAfter the exam...\n\nThe five friends walked toward the old oak tree.\n\nIt had become their favorite meeting place.\n\nMarcus dropped onto the grass.\n\n\"I think my brain officially resigned today.\"\n\nChloe smiled.\n\n\"You say that after every exam.\"\n\n\"And every semester I'm right.\"\n\nThe group burst into laughter.\n\nFor a few precious minutes...\n\nThere were no rumors.\n\nNo investigation.\n\nJust five friends enjoying each other's company.\n\nMeanwhile...\n\nDaniel met with Nathan Ross.\n\nNathan looked nervous but cooperative.\n\n\"You wanted to see me?\"\n\nDaniel nodded.\n\n\"You worked as a student assistant in the Research Archive.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Did you ever notice anything unusual before the documents disappeared?\"\n\nNathan thought carefully.\n\n\"There was one thing.\"\n\nDaniel leaned forward.\n\n\"What was it?\"\n\n\"Someone kept requesting temporary access cards.\"\n\n\"At first I thought it was normal.\"\n\n\"But later...\"\n\n\"It started happening almost every week.\"\n\nDaniel wrote it down.\n\n\"Who approved those requests?\"\n\nNathan frowned.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"They always arrived with the correct authorization.\"\n\n\"But I never met the person collecting them.\"\n\nDaniel thanked Nathan and watched him leave.\n\nTemporary access cards.\n\nThat explained something.\n\nWhoever framed Ariana might not have relied on one method.\n\nThey had created backups.\n\nAlternative routes.\n\nEscape plans.\n\nThis scheme had been built layer by layer.\n\nThat evening...\n\nAriana sat alone in the library.\n\nShe closed her textbook and looked out the window.\n\nGraduation was only a few weeks away.\n\nInstead of feeling excited...\n\nShe felt uncertain.\n\nWould people always remember her as the girl under investigation?\n\nOr would the truth finally set her free?\n\nShe quietly whispered to herself,\n\n\"I won't let this be the end of my story.\"\n\nAcross campus...\n\nDaniel opened another old maintenance file.\n\nA small yellow sticky note fell from between the pages.\n\nOnly four words were written on it.\n\nCheck visitor log again.\n\nNo signature.\n\nNo date.\n\nDaniel frowned.\n\nHe was certain he hadn't seen it before.\n\nWho had left the reminder?\n\nAnd why now?\n\n💭 Ending Quote\n\"Sometimes the smallest reminder points toward the biggest answer.\"   📒"
